VIG = "Mr R, a 52-year-old auto-rickshaw driver from Kanpur, drank about 90 g of alcohol daily for 20 years and stopped six months ago. He has no symptoms. Ultrasound for dyspepsia showed a nodular liver and a 14 cm spleen. Platelets are 98 × 10⁹/L, bilirubin 1.4 mg/dL, INR 1.3, creatinine 0.9 mg/dL and sodium 136 mmol/L. Liver stiffness is 31 kPa. He asks whether he needs a 'camera test' and whether there is anything he can do to stay well."

EXP = [
 explain("pathophysiology", "Why portal pressure drives the outcome", "Why does a patient with normal liver function tests still carry a high risk?",
  "Liver function tests measure hepatocyte injury and synthetic capacity, but they do not measure portal pressure. In cirrhosis, fibrosis and regenerative nodules distort the sinusoids and raise intrahepatic resistance, and blood flow into the portal system rises at the same time because the splanchnic vessels dilate. Once the hepatic venous pressure gradient reaches 10 mmHg, collaterals open, varices form, and the kidneys start retaining sodium. This threshold is called clinically significant portal hypertension (CSPH). It is the strongest predictor of the first decompensation — ascites, variceal bleeding or encephalopathy — and decompensation, not the cirrhosis label itself, is what shortens life.\nThis is why the modern approach targets portal pressure early. Carvedilol lowers portal inflow and, through alpha-1 blockade, intrahepatic resistance. Started when CSPH is likely, it lowers the risk of decompensation as well as bleeding. Removing the cause (alcohol, hepatitis B or C) also lowers portal pressure over time and can lead to clinical recompensation."),
 explain("signs-symptoms", "Recognising the patient who has changed", "Which findings at a routine visit mean the patient is no longer compensated?",
  "Most patients with compensated cirrhosis look well, so the task at each visit is to look for the transition. New ascites (weight gain, shifting dullness, leg swelling), any black stool or haematemesis, subtle encephalopathy (sleep reversal, poor concentration, a change noticed by family, asterixis) and new jaundice each signal decompensation and change the plan. Ask specifically about alcohol, over-the-counter painkillers and sedatives, because these precipitate decompensation and are common in India. Muscle wasting is easy to miss in a patient with ascites; check the temples and thighs and ask about protein intake. A patient who decompensates should be treated for the complication, reviewed for transplant, and have the precipitant identified."),
 explain("investigations", "Staging without invasive tests", "How do you decide who needs endoscopy and who needs a beta-blocker?",
  "Elastography and the platelet count do most of the staging. A liver stiffness of 25 kPa or more makes CSPH very likely (Baveno VII summary), so the patient gains from a beta-blocker whether or not varices are present; NICE does not offer screening endoscopy to people planning to take carvedilol or propranolol (1.2.6). When stiffness is below 20 kPa and platelets are above 150 × 10⁹/L, the chance of varices needing treatment is low and endoscopy can be skipped, with repeat elastography and platelets at follow-up. These rules apply to compensated cirrhosis; decompensated patients need endoscopy, and a patient whose beta-blocker is stopped needs yearly endoscopy (AASLD). Patients between these groups, or those who cannot take a beta-blocker, need endoscopy to find medium or large varices, which then need either a beta-blocker or band ligation. Separately, every patient with cirrhosis needs an ultrasound, with or without AFP, every 6 months for HCC, and a MELD score every 6 months; a MELD of 12 or more marks high risk and should prompt specialist referral."),
 explain("management", "Putting the long-term plan together", "What does a complete cirrhosis plan contain?",
  "A complete plan has five parts. First, treat the cause: support abstinence, start tenofovir or entecavir for hepatitis B, and treat hepatitis C with sofosbuvir and velpatasvir, because removing the cause can stop progression. Second, lower portal pressure: carvedilol starting at 6.25 mg a day and aiming for 12.5 mg a day, reducing the dose if systolic BP falls below 90 mmHg and avoiding it in refractory ascites. Third, surveillance: 6-monthly ultrasound and MELD, and endoscopy only when indicated. Fourth, prevent complications: salt restriction and diuretics for ascites, antibiotic prophylaxis only for high-risk patients, lactulose after an episode of encephalopathy with rifaximin added for recurrence, and strict avoidance of NSAIDs and sedatives. Fifth, protect the patient: adequate energy and protein with a late-evening snack, hepatitis B vaccination if non-immune, and early transplant referral when MELD rises or the patient decompensates.")]

